+ Will refund</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▼ does not recommend · 0.2 hrs · 2026-05-11</div><div class="body">Ah, Kenshi.  I purchased this game after watching numerous Youtube videos of people doing their own creative / fun &quot;runs&quot; (such as a slave to business owner run, or a farming run, etc).  Each looked pretty neat, and as an avid Rimworld fan I thought &quot;Hey, this is sort of like Rimworld but in third person 3d!&quot;.  Next steam sale, I excitedly hit purchase.
+
+ Well, I stand here now having installed the game and tried it for myself, and... &quot;underwhelmed&quot; doesn&#x27;t do it justice.  Have you ever played really old MMO&#x27;s, like EverQuest or DAOC?  Imagine hopping back in now, same graphics and all, and there was no tutorial or training missions to at least familiarize yourself with the controls.  Great, I did it to myself again, I purchased yet another game where I&#x27;m expected to watch hours of Youtube videos for stuff that the game itself refuses to teach.
+
+ There seems like there&#x27;s potential here, if you want a super sandboxy open world type thing where there isn&#x27;t any sort of story, plot, or world events.  It does seem to be one of those &quot;Everything and the kitchen sink, too&quot; sort of games, where if you have an idea - you can accomplish it.  I&#x27;m just typically not that creative, and need at least SOME degree of direction  to start playing a game. 
+
+ I may revise my review in the future if I give it another try, but I fear that this may be one of those games that&#x27;s going to be more interesting to watch other people play, than to actually play myself.  Maybe I&#x27;m missing something, but my brain was practically SCREAMING at me, &quot;Now what?&quot;.
+
+ **Update with more detail**
+ Gave it one more quick shot since I&#x27;m a bit of a masochist / hate wasting money on something I just purchased.  I genuinely feel like I&#x27;m missing something crucial to having fun here, because both times I got dropped into the &quot;Wanderer&quot; start - I felt like a toddler who was abandoned in the middle of an airport; confused, lost, directionless, and perhaps a bit scared.  After wandering aimlessly around the starting are…</div></div></details></div><div class="claim"><p class="text">Reviewers describe the game as lacking a tutorial or story mode to explain core mechanics and controls, leaving players unguided.</p><details><summary><span class="tag mono">▸ 4 reviews</span><span class="cta">Show receipts</span></summary><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▼ does not recommend · 0.8 hrs · 2026-07-05</div><div class="body">The game really needs a story mode to teach you how to play the game.  I don&#x27;t have the patience to spend a dozen hours just trying to learn the basics.</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 1.2 hrs · 2026-06-10</div><div class="body"># Kenshi — A Review
+
+ **Developer:** Lo-Fi Games | **Genre:** Open-World Sandbox RPG | **Platform:** PC
+
+ ---
+
+ There is a moment early in Kenshi — maybe your third or fourth hour in — where you realize the game is never going to hold your hand. Your character has just been beaten unconscious by a wandering band of bandits, left bleeding in the dirt of a sun-scorched wasteland, with no quest marker telling you where to go and no tutorial pop-up offering consolation. And somehow, impossibly, you can&#x27;t wait to get back up.
+
+ Kenshi is not a game of questlines. It&#x27;s a game of emergent stories — of being a shopkeeper and a thief, a lone wanderer and a slave, and eventually an entire community of people working together to one day erect a city in the wasteland. The game never scripted any of it. It just gave you a world and stepped back.
+
+ At its core, Kenshi is an open-world RPG with real-time strategy elements and no linear narrative, set in a post-apocalyptic world where survival is brutally difficult. You begin with no skills and struggle simply to stay alive, leveling up abilities through use — thievery by stealing, combat by fighting — and slowly recruiting others to grow your band.
+
+ Eventually it becomes a surprisingly effective management game as you research technologies, construct buildings, and assign your people long lists of automated tasks — mining, farming, construction. The scope of what you can build toward is staggering. The path to get there is unforgiving.
+
+ That difficulty is the game&#x27;s defining trait and its biggest barrier. The concept of a free-roaming RPG where you craft your own story is always intriguing, and the world is vast and impressive for a small indie project, but the game does a poor job of communicating its intricate systems to new players. There is no onboarding. You are expected to fail, learn, and try again. Whether that sounds like a feature or a flaw will determine whether Kenshi is for you.
+
