+ Eventually, the confusion starts to give way to…</div></div></details></div></div></section><section class="cohort"><h2>20-100h</h2><div class="stats mono">20-100 hours · 93.7% positive, based on 441 reviews</div><p class="summary">Mid-game adventurers rely heavily on community mods to manage difficult mechanics and rough pathfinding.</p><div class="theme"><h3>Difficulty</h3><div class="claim"><p class="text">Reviewers note that the game features a steep learning curve and a severe lack of an in-game tutorial or direction for new players.</p><details><summary><span class="tag mono">▸ 3 reviews</span><span class="cta">Show receipts</span></summary><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 37.1 hrs · 2026-07-17</div><div class="body">Kenshi is a surprisingly deep and engaging game. Great progression that&#x27;s individual for every character, (although brutally difficult in the beginning), a dauntingly huge map with an absurdly huge amount of choices for where to go.
+
+ I do however have 2 negatives to bring up. With the Sci-Fi setting not having any kind of firearms is a hugely missed opportunity and feels like they should be there, but it&#x27;s missing. And for new players there&#x27;s a lack of direction and tutorial in my opinion. You&#x27;re thrown into the world and get a figurative pat on the back and a &quot;You&#x27;re out here now, good luck!&quot;.
+
+ Some more explained details on the world, the in game mechanics and a more interactive tutorial wouldn&#x27;t go amiss. But besides these 2 things I don&#x27;t really have any complaints about it, except that I would personally like if the dialogue was voice acted. It does what it&#x27;s supposed to incredibly well, the mod support is amazing with many fun additions you can throw into the game quite easily.
+
+ I was a bit sceptical at first since the gameplay looked more action oriented than it is, and the game doesn&#x27;t have controller support. But a good way to describe the controls is Kotor, but with less combat options, base building and a larger party system.
+
+ Overall I give Kenshi 8.5/10. If guns (natively, not through mods), a longer and interactive tutorial, and voice acting are added it&#x27;ll easily become a full 10/10!</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 24.8 hrs · 2026-05-26</div><div class="body">There is a very steep learning curve and I&#x27;m still figuring it out with systems I haven&#x27;t even touched yet, but I can say this game has an insane amount of depth to it along with some jank. The leveling system is unique in that you have skill levels, not character levels that increase according to where those stats are relative to whatever you are fighting/doing. The cool part is that you can manipulate this with various temporary stat buffs/debuffs. So it becomes a balancing act of how can I game the system for maximum stat gains without dying or being too inefficient. 
+
+ There are plenty of things that might seem like a negative or bad, like losing a limb or getting knocked out, enslaved, shotting squamates, etc, but you can game that for gain as well. 
+
+ Learning how things work and progression feels really rewarding, worth a try if you are on the fence.</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 52.3 hrs · 2026-05-16</div><div class="body">Kenshi is the only game where being completely demolished qualifies as character growth.
+ You begin as weak as a wet paper towel, get mugged by homeless people trying to survive, captured by slavers, and gobbled up alive by fauna that could have come from a bad dream, yet still you wind up feeling:
+ “Yeah… one more hour.”
+ The absolute highlight is the tutorial.
+ In most games, the tutorial teaches you how to play.
+ In Kenshi, it’s literally just “good luck buddy,” throwing you into the desert without clothes.
+ 10/10, best stockholm syndrome simulation.</div></div></details></div><div class="claim"><p class="text">Reviewers describe starting out exceptionally weak and prone to being defeated, captured, or enslaved early in the game.</p><details><summary><span class="tag mono">▸ 3 reviews</span><span class="cta">Show receipts</span></summary><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 30.9 hrs · 2026-07-29</div><div class="body">This game really wants you to die and get beat up for your race. Get beaten up and bleed out in a desert while hoping you don&#x27;t get enslaved or eaten by some animal. I have played for 30 hours and have yet to go a day without thinking it will be my last, im gay. 10/10 you gotta play this. ALSO the npcs are racist</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 31.1 hrs · 2026-07-11</div><div class="body">This game is genuinely one of a kind. It&#x27;s a little bit janky and buggy at times, but the minor issues are 100% worth looking past for the unique brutal splendor that is this game.
+
+ I started as a woman in a nation that hates women. Told someone to go ♥♥♥♥ themself for being a misogynist and within 10 minutes of starting Id had the ♥♥♥♥ beaten out of me and was enslaved. Got brought to a camp and bided my time. Made my escape by freeing a few other slaves, only one made it but she joined my team and when my main character got knocked unconscious she carried me to safety till we could find medicine. 20 hours later theres six of us living in a big ass house in a town while we research and practice smithing. I&#x27;m gonna go back and burn the nation that enslaved me to the ground when I can.
+
+ This might genuinely be one of my new favorite games and I regret waiting so long to buy it. I love it. Dont be me, buy it now. 10/10</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 52.3 hrs · 2026-05-16</div><div class="body">Kenshi is the only game where being completely demolished qualifies as character growth.
