  Will refund</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▼ does not recommend · 1.5 hrs · 2026-05-27</div><div class="body">I&#x27;m the sort of person that should love an open game like this. I loved Morrowind, I loved Warband, and I&#x27;ve played games just like this on Kongregate back in the day (more recommendations are very welcome!). But wow... this just completely misses for me.
  
  There&#x27;s almost entirely no sound at all. No music, no sfx. You just right click areas to do stuff, rather than feeling like you&#x27;re actually playing a game. There&#x27;s no quests, and no story. 
  
  So far, I walk over to the iron, mine the iron, walk to town to sell, buy some stuff for my outpost, walk to outpost, wait for build, then walk back to iron, repeat. That loop takes like 15 minutes, and considering you just right click on the map to move, almost the ENTIRE 15 minutes is literally just me staring at the screen do it&#x27;s thing, with zero input. Realistically, this is almost an idler game. However, if you do in fact idle and go AFK, some random little thing will attack you, and since you don&#x27;t give inputs (or it&#x27;s too strong) you just lose. Losing &quot;can&quot; be as simple as being injured and needing to hobble back to town, or, as in my one case, you&#x27;re unconscious, with no food or medicine, so you can&#x27;t do anything at all to help yourself, and you just die. Gameover, because it&#x27;s permadeath. So you&#x27;re forced to sit there watching the painfully long walk, the extremely painfully long mining process, etc. I&#x27;m sure there&#x27;s good elements to this game, it looks super promising, but WOW it&#x27;s boring to actually sit through, and you MUST sit through it, or else keep making backup saves in case it goes bad.
  
  I&#x27;m still really wanting to get into it, because I can see there&#x27;s a gem here, but it&#x27;s a diamond in the rough... a lot of rough... and I just don&#x27;t want to subject myself to painfully boring torturously bad gameplay, just for that little nugget of goodness.</div></div></details></div><div class="claim"><p class="text">Reviewers describe the game controls as janky, clunky, and poorly adapted for controllers.</p><details><summary><span class="tag mono">▸ 3 reviews</span><span class="cta">Show receipts</span></summary><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▼ does not recommend · 1.0 hrs · 2026-06-01</div><div class="body">I really did try to love Kenshi. I watched ambiguous amphibian&#x27;s playthroughs, watched a few hours worths of tips and tricks for noobs and none of it helped. Here&#x27;s a couple of gripes that I have only having played an hour(mainly cuz I wanted to give myself the choice of refunding).

  1st gripe:
  If you&#x27;re planning to play this game with a controller(or on a steam deck in my case), don&#x27;t bother. All the tutorials and help this game gives you all assume you&#x27;re playing on a keyboard. I&#x27;m no stranger to weird and clunky controls clearly meant for a keyboard and haphazardly being mapped to a controller, but this one just takes the cake. If you&#x27;re forced to play on a controller on this game, you&#x27;re basically screwed. Even moreso than the people who play this game on their pc. 

  2nd gripe:
  The game is very unintuitive. Now I&#x27;ve played some hella unintuitive games before(funger &amp; funger 2, coe 5, and rimworld), but this one just takes it to a whole nother level that is frankly unfun.

  When I try to form up a squad(like the tutorial blurb told me to), I get shot with a 6K price tag from john gunsmith. When I finally stumble unto the merchant after looking at a barren dessert and 30 minutes of fighting with the controls, the funds that I started as a wanderer may as well have been 0. When I try to build a small shack(cuz that&#x27;s what the very first tutorial blurb i read outside of the pc controls ones said I needed to do to build a research bench), I get hit with &quot;it&#x27;s too close to a town&quot;, &quot;this building needs 15K&quot;, and when I manage to find a spot it required 5 building materials that cost an arm and a leg outside where there&#x27;s literally nothing. 

  All this work just to access the very first concept the game taught me which was a research bench. I am sure it&#x27;s better on pc, but labelling this game as &quot;playable&quot; on the steam deck oled is just false.

  In conclusion, I really did want to give this game a chance. The community around it has made some amazing content and has told …</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▼ does not recommend · 1.1 hrs · 2026-05-31</div><div class="body">Nothing is explained (correctly) to you, and even if it were, it happens after it&#x27;s needed (e.g. combat tutorial appearing after you die, healing tutorial appearing only after you&#x27;ve healed yourself like wtf??)
