  **Developer:** Lo-Fi Games | **Genre:** Open-World Sandbox RPG | **Platform:** PC

  ---

  There is a moment early in Kenshi — maybe your third or fourth hour in — where you realize the game is never going to hold your hand. Your character has just been beaten unconscious by a wandering band of bandits, left bleeding in the dirt of a sun-scorched wasteland, with no quest marker telling you where to go and no tutorial pop-up offering consolation. And somehow, impossibly, you can&#x27;t wait to get back up.

  Kenshi is not a game of questlines. It&#x27;s a game of emergent stories — of being a shopkeeper and a thief, a lone wanderer and a slave, and eventually an entire community of people working together to one day erect a city in the wasteland. The game never scripted any of it. It just gave you a world and stepped back.

  At its core, Kenshi is an open-world RPG with real-time strategy elements and no linear narrative, set in a post-apocalyptic world where survival is brutally difficult. You begin with no skills and struggle simply to stay alive, leveling up abilities through use — thievery by stealing, combat by fighting — and slowly recruiting others to grow your band.

  Eventually it becomes a surprisingly effective management game as you research technologies, construct buildings, and assign your people long lists of automated tasks — mining, farming, construction. The scope of what you can build toward is staggering. The path to get there is unforgiving.

  That difficulty is the game&#x27;s defining trait and its biggest barrier. The concept of a free-roaming RPG where you craft your own story is always intriguing, and the world is vast and impressive for a small indie project, but the game does a poor job of communicating its intricate systems to new players. There is no onboarding. You are expected to fail, learn, and try again. Whether that sounds like a feature or a flaw will determine whether Kenshi is for you.

