  In most games, the tutorial teaches you how to play.
  In Kenshi, it’s literally just “good luck buddy,” throwing you into the desert without clothes.
  10/10, best stockholm syndrome simulation.</div></div></details></div></div><div class="theme"><h3>Performance</h3><div class="claim"><p class="text">Reviewers rely on the extensive modding community and Steam Workshop to fix bugs, improve performance, and add quality-of-life adjustments.</p><details><summary><span class="tag mono">▸ 5 reviews</span><span class="cta">Show receipts</span></summary><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 60.1 hrs · 2026-07-28</div><div class="body">Please flesh out the automation a bit more, I highly recommend officially adopting the kenshi extension module that the modding community uses and the fixes they provide, there is no point in not accepting their contribution. By making the modding more integrated and unified, you can more easily provide a surface for extensions, like minecraft does with its mod authors, your aim is to effectively provide an ontology. The current hierarchy of your object graph is already very good in structure, refinements exist primarily as performance improvement, if you ran this in my companies custom ETCS (Entity Tag Component System) runtime, you could see events updating on the order of 10 million events per second +, as the system retains partial order over type graphs for you through C++ meta templating (CRTP). Since we are considering releasing our engine under open source (LGPL), you may be in for an interesting surprise in the future! I&#x27;ll update this once its released, and if your interested, you can use it (as it is open source).</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 60.4 hrs · 2026-07-27</div><div class="body">This game is AWESOME. I never find games that hold my attention for more than 2 weeks, but I&#x27;ve been playing this for months. A bit unoptimized, but mods fix this!</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 56.8 hrs · 2026-07-26</div><div class="body">Kenshi is definitely one of those games that you don&#x27;t realize how fun it is until you play. The in game world is vast and unique. It definitely keeps the gameplay refreshing as you travel around. I personally love the health / combat system, and I feel like skill leveling is balanced perfectly. Skills don&#x27;t level too fast but are just fast enough for you to feel like progress is happening.  The steam workshop and nexus both host a massive selection of well-made mods to tweak the game as you&#x27;d like, and I&#x27;ve seen some pretty amazing things recently on that front. The performance out-of-the-box is mildly annoying but it can be mitigated with a few mods.</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 94.6 hrs · 2026-07-18</div><div class="body">There’s really nothing else quite like it. Starting with a handful of useless nobodies and eventually building a powerful squad, a functioning base, and enough confidence to fight entire factions is incredibly satisfying. The freedom to play however you want is easily the game’s biggest strength, although the late game can feel a little empty once your squad is strong, your research is mostly complete, and you’ve run out of meaningful goals to chase.

  That said, Kenshi is held together with duct tape and hope. The bugs can be frustrating, the pathfinding regularly makes terrible decisions, and managing a large squad sometimes feels like supervising a field trip where everyone is actively trying to get lost.

  Mods also feel almost required for the best experience. Quality-of-life improvements, world-state mods, UI changes, and bug fixes smooth out a lot of rough edges that probably should have been handled by the base game.

  Kenshi is clunky, ugly, confusing, and occasionally infuriating, but it is also genuinely unique and very hard to put down once it clicks.</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 38.2 hrs · 2026-05-26</div><div class="body">It is an interesting take of a old western japanese steam punk medieval fantasy sword fantasy kung fu post apocalyptic sci fi setting. I&#x27;ve never seen someone mash this many ideas and just get a setting that feels cohesive and immersive. So the world building is good, now the gameplay, that needs work. Fortunately, this is one of the most moddable games out there, reducing raid frequencies, increasing NPCs, improving combat, rebalancing the economy... And now with Re Kenshi it is getting to another level again.</div></div></details></div><div class="claim"><p class="text">Reviewers report that the pathfinding AI struggles with obstacles like building hitboxes, occasionally causing characters to freeze or run in circles.</p><details><summary><span class="tag mono">▸ 3 reviews</span><span class="cta">Show receipts</span></summary><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 94.6 hrs · 2026-07-18</div><div class="body">There’s really nothing else quite like it. Starting with a handful of useless nobodies and eventually building a powerful squad, a functioning base, and enough confidence to fight entire factions is incredibly satisfying. The freedom to play however you want is easily the game’s biggest strength, although the late game can feel a little empty once your squad is strong, your research is mostly complete, and you’ve run out of meaningful goals to chase.

  That said, Kenshi is held together with duct tape and hope. The bugs can be frustrating, the pathfinding regularly makes terrible decisions, and managing a large squad sometimes feels like supervising a field trip where everyone is actively trying to get lost.

  Mods also feel almost required for the best experience. Quality-of-life improvements, world-state mods, UI changes, and bug fixes smooth out a lot of rough edges that probably should have been handled by the base game.

  Kenshi is clunky, ugly, confusing, and occasionally infuriating, but it is also genuinely unique and very hard to put down once it clicks.</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▼ does not recommend · 79.2 hrs · 2026-07-17</div><div class="body">Incredible game when it functions correctly. Unfortunately I don&#x27;t think I&#x27;ve had a single play session so far that wasn&#x27;t interrupted for several minutes because of a bug or some jank ass feature. Corpes go flying miles away more often than not. Characters freeze and refuse to move until you reload. Any character doing an action autonomously stops when it&#x27;s tile isn&#x27;t being rendered, and the pathfinding rarely works as intended. There are also graphical issues and pop-in even at max settings, which I wouldn&#x27;t usually mention but it does become a problem when you&#x27;re trying to identify a building on the horizon.
  
  What I find so frustrating is that the game is set up to give you control of large groups of units and lets you give them jobs to do without your input. This idea is completely worthless however when your stupid little ♥♥♥♥♥♥♥ can&#x27;t go 5 minutes without getting caught on the hitbox of a building.
  
  There&#x27;s so much potential here. Without considering bugs I would probably give it an 8.5/10. I just find myself frustrated too often to forgive.</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▼ does not recommend · 33.5 hrs · 2026-05-04</div><div class="body">I so want to recommend this game, it&#x27;s fun, both playing in combat and trading, but the pathing AI is hot garbage. You will have to micromange any pathing beyond LOS or else you&#x27;ll suddenly find they decided not to go the same path they have the last 10 times, but instead went on a new path or just decided to stand still and give up letting any skin spider or starving bandit wipe your entire squad. Not so bad when you have one squad or 1 person, but if you want to do any base building you may as well just have everyone sit in the middle of town twiddling their thumbs or else you&#x27;ll come back to find them running in circles.</div></div></details></div></div></section><section class="cohort"><h2>100h+</h2><div class="stats mono">over 100 hours · 98.0% positive, based on 458 reviews</div><p class="summary">Veterans embrace the brutal, unguided opening in exchange for massive sandbox freedom and emergent gameplay.</p><div class="theme"><h3>Difficulty</h3><div class="claim"><p class="text">Reviewers describe a harsh, brutal, and unguided opening experience with a steep learning curve and slow progress, lacking hand-holding or tutorials.</p><details><summary><span class="tag mono">▸ 5 reviews</span><span class="cta">Show receipts</span></summary><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 1226.3 hrs · 2026-07-25</div><div class="body">An excellent game for those who want real hardcore and aren&#x27;t afraid of very slow progress during gameplay. A brutal game that doesn&#x27;t care about you. You can be a loner or have your own squad. Play as an assassin, an organization with goals you choose yourself (Role Play), be a trader, a vigilante, a warlord, a bounty hunter, a ranger organization that helps people, a drug lord, a slaver, an anti-slaver.

  The game shines when you, after you become comfortable with the game, pick a role in the world of Kenshi.

  Mods are strongly recommended</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 228.4 hrs · 2026-07-23</div><div class="body">Very steep learning curve here. If you&#x27;re into instant gratification, this one might not be for you. But, if you love working your ass off for a very satisfying payoff, this one is worth it.</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 222.7 hrs · 2026-07-22</div><div class="body">THIS GAME IS AMAZING. I have never played something with so much sandbox freedom, so much space to move around, places to visit, goals to make, things to do. This game does not hold your hand, and they weren&#x27;t kidding!

  I first heard about Kenshi from Ssethzeentach and at first didn&#x27;t think much of it. Though there came a time when I wanted to experience something new, and I remembered Kenshi with his high recommendation.

  Ever since then I have loved playing this game, creating several different starts and living out different gameplay and stories, each unique and different from the last, even if it&#x27;s on the same Moon of Kenshi. I too, recommend this game. Thank you Lo-Fi Games and Chris Hunt!</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 378.8 hrs · 2026-07-19</div><div class="body">Are there any bugs? F*ck yeah, there&#x27;s tons of em. Do i care? Nope. Does the game look like ass? It sure does. Do i care? Again, nope. Is the game janky as hell? It sure is. Yet again, do i care? No, i don&#x27;t.
  The first few hours are rough, because you have to get over all of the jank and unpolished things. 
  But once you do, you won&#x27;t be able to put it down.
  There is NOTHING else like it. It&#x27;s an RPG where you and your characters make the story.
  You choose what you want to do. There are no quests, there are no markers, nothing.
  Every playthrough is different and i urge you to not savescum. Roll with the punches, see what happens. Make it your own unique story. It will always be different.
  I bought the game at full price shortly after a sale (bad timing on my part) because i watched a couple of videos and i thought it looked really cool and i was bored.
  At first i regretted my purchase, to be quite honest.
  But just a few hours later, i forgot all about it.
  At this point, i feel like i robbed the dev.
  Yes, you read that right. Dev. This was mostly developed by a single guy at first.
  I can&#x27;t wait for Kenshi 2. 
  If you can get it in a sale and you are just slightly interested, BUY IT!
  if it&#x27;s not on sale and you are interested, BUY IT!
  You won&#x27;t regret it. Just give it more than 10 minutes. This isn&#x27;t your typical AAA slop that hits you with dopamine injections every couple of minutes. 
  Instead it rewards patience, creativity and persistence.
  This is a game where you can truly create your own story in a massive sandbox.
  But after a couple more hours, i didn&#x27;t anymore</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 270.3 hrs · 2026-04-29</div><div class="body">This games got to be one of the most in-depth reactive sandbox game I have encountered. It&#x27;s a real gaming gem in the rough, I wish it had more features, but the amount of mods should cover most of that. There is  also Kenshi 2 that&#x27;s gonna come up eventually so something to look forward to.

  If you are on the fence about buying this game know these things, this doesn&#x27;g have a story or gameplay tutorial is not gonna help you at all, it&#x27;s not sameful to look things up, and it&#x27;s gonna be a very rough start, but once you start understanding how to get what you want, this game becomes very enloyble.</div></div></details></div><div class="claim"><p class="text">Reviewers report that the game lacks a tutorial or hand-holding systems for new players.</p><details><summary><span class="tag mono">▸ 2 reviews</span><span class="cta">Show receipts</span></summary><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 222.7 hrs · 2026-07-22</div><div class="body">THIS GAME IS AMAZING. I have never played something with so much sandbox freedom, so much space to move around, places to visit, goals to make, things to do. This game does not hold your hand, and they weren&#x27;t kidding!

  I first heard about Kenshi from Ssethzeentach and at first didn&#x27;t think much of it. Though there came a time when I wanted to experience something new, and I remembered Kenshi with his high recommendation.

  Ever since then I have loved playing this game, creating several different starts and living out different gameplay and stories, each unique and different from the last, even if it&#x27;s on the same Moon of Kenshi. I too, recommend this game. Thank you Lo-Fi Games and Chris Hunt!</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 270.3 hrs · 2026-04-29</div><div class="body">This games got to be one of the most in-depth reactive sandbox game I have encountered. It&#x27;s a real gaming gem in the rough, I wish it had more features, but the amount of mods should cover most of that. There is  also Kenshi 2 that&#x27;s gonna come up eventually so something to look forward to.

  If you are on the fence about buying this game know these things, this doesn&#x27;g have a story or gameplay tutorial is not gonna help you at all, it&#x27;s not sameful to look things up, and it&#x27;s gonna be a very rough start, but once you start understanding how to get what you want, this game becomes very enloyble.</div></div></details></div></div><div class="theme"><h3>Content</h3><div class="claim"><p class="text">Reviewers note that the game features extensive sandbox freedom with no mandatory story quests or markers, allowing players to create their own roles, build bases, and shape unique narratives.</p><details><summary><span class="tag mono">▸ 5 reviews</span><span class="cta">Show receipts</span></summary><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 1000.2 hrs · 2026-07-24</div><div class="body">Very steep learning curve, can feel overwhelming and a bit unfinished/unpolished but once you understand the basic mecanics it becomes a true sandbox with lore in which you can write your own story fully the way you want. 

  For example, my frist few playthroughs were just surviving and getting by with a small gang trying to evade wildlife and slavers while looking for a safe place to setup a small base and a farm. The funny thing is I eventually figured out is that there is no safe place in the world of kenshi, when picking a location for a base the game forces you to consider which threats you are willing/can deal with and which you would rather avoid or cannot deal with. Playing as an adventurer / trader solo or with a small group without a base completely changes the gameplay as you will need to approach the game differently and will face different challanges and threats while traveling.

  Fast forward to current playthorugh, I am waging war on one of the major factions, harrasing their patrols, inflitrating their cities, stealing tech and valuables, and kidnapping leaders and nobles to  destabilize their cities. The captives I then offer to their enemy (Shek &amp; Skeletons) and that way I am forming alliances to form combined attacks and takeovers of their cities, one by one, untill the faction is completely shattered and no longer a threat. And after that its on to the next faction.

  In case you are wondering, my campaign list is the Holy Nation &gt; Smaller slaver factions and the slave market &gt; the United Cities and the Traders Guild &gt; Cannibals, bandits, hostile hive&#x27;s, fogmen, thralls, and eventually march a massive army to crush CatLon and whatever is left of his empire.

  Then there is the workshop with mods ranging from simple content additions to mods that completely overhaul the game, mechanics, combat, factions, and more lore. There are DLC quality and content level overhauls like Genesis for example!

  Wrote this at 999.7 hours in and I dont even know what pl…</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 176.9 hrs · 2026-07-21</div><div class="body">Honestly, been on the fence for a long time on this one.  Its a fun sandbox game where you see what the world has to offer and just enjoy playing.  No achievements to fuss over, no specific story to push you in one direction.  It has been a long time since I&#x27;ve played a game that was just a fun game to explore and enjoy.  It does have a few things I personally have issues with, but 90% of those can be solved by looking up a few mods the community has created to make the gameplay more suited to what you prefer.  Honestly a fun game and worth its price if you enjoy an open sandbox game.
  
  For example with the modding, one issue I had was the speed in which you learn skills.  It felt a little slow to me, so I simply got a mod that doubled it.</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 137.1 hrs · 2026-07-19</div><div class="body">Easily one of my top games of 2026 despite getting close to being 8 years old. I cannot wait for Kenshi 2 and whenever I decide to have a go at another playthrough.

  Kenshi is an amazing open world game that really managed to sink its claws into me. If you love the idea of a top-down, open world sandbox game where the story of your playthrough is fully up to you: Wanna be a slave that plots to escape to freedom? A wandering trader who eventually makes a trading outpost? A warrior looking for the next big challenge? Pro Slavery? Anti-Slavery? 

  All the choices are in the palm of your hand as you are fully free to focus on a chill/base building experience, go full murder/combat, explore and everywhere in between. You can further change the experience with the advanced settings, whether you want the game to be even more harsh than it can be or if you want it to be more chill. In either case the choice is fully yours. 

  I have had plenty of memorable moments both when I was playing with just my MC and when I finally got around to recruiting people both for combat and for building a base. The game runs smooth like butter even at 4K and with visual mods. Speaking of mods you can further enhance the experience with the amazing visual, performance enhancing &amp; QoL mods the community has created over the years. Feel free to look through my mod list if you want some ideas of what you can add to the game.

  Overall between the base game and what the community has built, Kenshi is an amazing open world sandbox game that I think anyone interested in these styles of games should give a try. The game might kick your ass but you will have a grand time whether you are getting yours handed to you, doing the handing, just exploring or building a base. Kenshi will surely have something you can do that appeals to your style of gameplay in an open world game.</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 378.8 hrs · 2026-07-19</div><div class="body">Are there any bugs? F*ck yeah, there&#x27;s tons of em. Do i care? Nope. Does the game look like ass? It sure does. Do i care? Again, nope. Is the game janky as hell? It sure is. Yet again, do i care? No, i don&#x27;t.
  The first few hours are rough, because you have to get over all of the jank and unpolished things. 
  But once you do, you won&#x27;t be able to put it down.
  There is NOTHING else like it. It&#x27;s an RPG where you and your characters make the story.
  You choose what you want to do. There are no quests, there are no markers, nothing.
  Every playthrough is different and i urge you to not savescum. Roll with the punches, see what happens. Make it your own unique story. It will always be different.
  I bought the game at full price shortly after a sale (bad timing on my part) because i watched a couple of videos and i thought it looked really cool and i was bored.
  At first i regretted my purchase, to be quite honest.
  But just a few hours later, i forgot all about it.
  At this point, i feel like i robbed the dev.
  Yes, you read that right. Dev. This was mostly developed by a single guy at first.
  I can&#x27;t wait for Kenshi 2. 
  If you can get it in a sale and you are just slightly interested, BUY IT!
  if it&#x27;s not on sale and you are interested, BUY IT!
  You won&#x27;t regret it. Just give it more than 10 minutes. This isn&#x27;t your typical AAA slop that hits you with dopamine injections every couple of minutes. 
  Instead it rewards patience, creativity and persistence.
  This is a game where you can truly create your own story in a massive sandbox.
  But after a couple more hours, i didn&#x27;t anymore</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 394.4 hrs · 2026-05-25</div><div class="body">I sincerely congratulate Lo-Fi Games, and especially Chris Hunt, on reaching three million copies sold of Kenshi. It is a well-deserved result of a long journey-everything that was done was not in vain.
  Kenshi is one of those rare games that truly gives a sense of freedom, adventure, and your own unique story. Thank you for the incredible world, atmosphere, and hundreds of hours of unforgettable experiences. Wishing you continued success with the development of the sequel!</div></div></details></div></div><div class="theme"><h3>Performance</h3><div class="claim"><p class="text">Reviewers note that the game crashes when the screen times out while running.</p><details><summary><span class="tag mono">▸ 2 reviews</span><span class="cta">Show receipts</span></summary><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 136.3 hrs · 2026-07-25</div><div class="body">it lets you do alot except building underground or in the air or underwater quests are kinda punch and done no real city creating development like hospitals and baby making or shop advertising or creating an environment to siphone people from other citys but i mean its pretty good wish the combat scaled to higher feats cause not being able to raid 5 citys and shove them all into my black hole pocket is a win but i can barely get 1 city then the stats get such high negatives the 2nd city isnt worth it i mean i modded the ♥♥♥♥ outta the game so it was more interesting but that only goes so far and it crashs my pc everytime my screen times out when the game is running so thats really annoying anyway thanks for the cool game im definitly gonna play it again later</div></div><div class="citation"><div class="meta mono">▲ recommends · 289.1 hrs · 2026-05-12</div><div class="body">It&#x27;s worth it. Jump in and get beaten to death by starving bandits. Jump back in to get [i] nearly [/i] beaten to death by bandits and become stronger. Become so strong you can 1v50 those bandits with your bare hands while naked and carrying the unconscious body of their leader then Naruto run that boy to the nearest city for a bounty.

  You can play this game in so many different ways that I keep coming back to try out a new idea for a playthrough. All of that is [b] before [/b] you dip your toes into the insanely dedicated modding community for this game over on the steam workshop.

  Is it janky? [b] OH MY YES [/b]

  Do you sometimes have to reload because your squad member glitched into a wall and you can&#x27;t get him out? [b] More frequently than you should, yes. [/b]

  Is it worth all the jank? [b] Without any doubt, yes. [/b]

- Kenshi is a gem of a game and I couldn&#x27;t be more excited to see what&#x27;s in store for Kenshi II.</div></div></details></div></div></section></main><footer class="mono verdict-footer">Data: 1,239 reviews across 4 cohorts · generated 2026-08-10 </footer></div>
